-- title:
--   Mixing Lemma, pp.28, 33 (corrected: both summands nonempty)
-- statement:
--   Let $\mathcal H=\mathcal H_1\oplus\mathcal H_2$ with orthonormal bases $\{|i_1\rangle\}$ of $\mathcal H_1$ and $\{|i_2\rangle\}$ of $\mathcal H_2$, both nonempty. If one can implement, for all $i_1,i_2$,
--   $$X_{i_1i_2}=|i_1\rangle\langle i_2|+|i_2\rangle\langle i_1|,\quad Y_{i_1i_2}=i|i_1\rangle\langle i_2|-i|i_2\rangle\langle i_1|,\quad Z_{i_1i_2}=|i_1\rangle\langle i_1|-|i_2\rangle\langle i_2|,$$
--   then one can implement $\mathfrak{su}(n_1+n_2)$ on $\mathcal H$: the real Lie algebra generated by $iX_{i_1i_2},iY_{i_1i_2},iZ_{i_1i_2}$ consists exactly of the $iA$ with $A$ Hermitian and traceless.
--
--   This is Step 3 of the proof: the B–T $\mathfrak{su}(2)$'s of Step 2 generate all of $\mathfrak{su}(n_J)$.
--
--   Differences from the source:
--   - correction: any dimensions n₁, n₂ → n₁, n₂ ≥ 1; reason: for n₁ = 0, n₂ = 2 there are no operators X, Y, Z, so the generated algebra is 0, not su(2)
--   - encoding: H₁ ⊕ H₂ with orthonormal bases → ℂ^ι with the standard basis split into S and its complement; reason: any finite-dimensional Hilbert space with such bases is unitarily identified with this one, and the statement is invariant
--   - reading: one can implement su(n₁ + n₂) → the generated real Lie algebra equals {iA : A Hermitian, tr A = 0}; reason: implementing a Hamiltonian A means iA lies in the real Lie closure (laws 1–3, p.11); 'generate su(n)' (Appendix C) is equality
-- source:
--   Kempe, Bacon, Lidar & Whaley, Theory of Decoherence-Free Fault-Tolerant Universal Quantum Computation, arXiv:quant-ph/0004064v2, Mixing Lemma, §VII.D.3, p.28; Appendix C, p.33

import Mathlib

namespace CollectiveDFS

attribute [local instance 100] LieRing.ofAssociativeRing

/-- The Mixing Lemma (Kempe, Bacon, Lidar & Whaley, arXiv:quant-ph/0004064v2, §VII.D.3, p.28, and Appendix C, p.33, PDF pp.28, 33): let
`H = H₁ ⊕ H₂` with orthonormal bases `{|i₁⟩}`, `{|i₂⟩}`. If one can implement
`X_{i₁i₂} = |i₁⟩⟨i₂| + |i₂⟩⟨i₁|`, `Y_{i₁i₂} = i|i₁⟩⟨i₂| − i|i₂⟩⟨i₁|`, `Z_{i₁i₂} = |i₁⟩⟨i₁| − |i₂⟩⟨i₂|`
for all `i₁, i₂`, then one can implement `su(n₁ + n₂)` on `H`. Here: the real Lie algebra
generated by `i X`, `i Y`, `i Z` over all `i₁ ∈ S`, `i₂ ∉ S` consists exactly of the `i A` with
`A` Hermitian and traceless.

**Formalization Note** `H = ℂ^ι` and the two orthonormal bases are the standard basis vectors
indexed by `S` and its complement (any pair of orthonormal bases is carried to these by a
unitary). `|i⟩⟨j|` is `Matrix.single i j 1`. Implementing a Hamiltonian `A` means `i A` lies in the
real Lie algebra (bracket the commutator, `[iA, iB] = i (i[A, B])`). Both blocks are assumed
nonempty. -/
theorem mixing_lemma {ι : Type*} [Fintype ι] [DecidableEq ι] (S : Set ι) (hS : S.Nonempty)
    (hSc : Sᶜ.Nonempty) (A : Matrix ι ι ℂ) :
    Complex.I • A ∈ LieSubalgebra.lieSpan ℝ (Matrix ι ι ℂ)
        {M | ∃ i ∈ S, ∃ j ∉ S,
          M = Complex.I • (Matrix.single i j (1 : ℂ) + Matrix.single j i 1) ∨
          M = Complex.I • (Complex.I • Matrix.single i j (1 : ℂ) - Complex.I • Matrix.single j i 1) ∨
          M = Complex.I • (Matrix.single i i (1 : ℂ) - Matrix.single j j 1)} ↔
      A.IsHermitian ∧ A.trace = 0 := by sorry

end CollectiveDFS
